    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for any confusion. Let's focus on the clinical aspects of insulin and glycemic index.\n\nInsulin is a hormone produced by the pancreas that regulates blood sugar levels. After you eat, carbohydrates in your food are broken down into glucose, which enters your bloodstream. Insulin helps cells throughout your body absorb glucose and use it for energy. When you consume high glycemic index (GI) foods, they cause a rapid increase in blood glucose levels. This leads to a significant release of insulin from the pancreas to help transport the glucose into cells.\n\nOver time, consistently high blood sugar levels and the resulting high insulin demand can lead to insulin resistance. Insulin resistance is a condition where the body's cells don't respond effectively to insulin, making it harder for glucose to enter cells and be used for energy. This can contribute to the development of type 2 diabetes.\n\nLow glycemic index (GI) foods, in contrast, cause a slower and more gradual rise in blood glucose levels. This results in a lower and more steady release of insulin, which can help maintain better blood sugar control and reduce the risk of insulin resistance.\n\nIn summary, choosing low GI foods can help manage blood sugar levels more effectively and reduce the risk of insulin resistance, which is beneficial for",
